{
  "language_code": "eng",
  "language_probability": 1.0,
  "text": "Follow the room air circuit. A crack or crawl unit sends cooled supply air to the equipment. The air absorbs heat as it passes through the equipment. Then warm return air travels back to the unit. At the coil, heat leaves the air. A crack unit transfers it into refrigerant. A crawl unit transfers it into chilled water. The connected system carries the heat onward for rejection outdoors. The cooled air returns to the supply side. Now compare a mixing path. Some warm exhaust can recirculate toward equipment inlets. Containment separates that exhaust from the supply stream and guides it toward the cooling unit instead. A barrier creates no cooling capacity. Verify equipment inlet conditions, adequate airflow, and working cooling controls",
